<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.5.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01316</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.5.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f7410746-0f68-4468-b13e-fd2818a1c889</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this
      lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>A new bicycle sells for $300. It is on sale for \(\frac14\) off the regular price. <emphasis>Select three</emphasis> expressions that represent the sale price of the bicycle in dollars. </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    \(300 \cdot \frac14\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(300 \cdot \frac34\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(300 \cdot (1-\frac{1}{4})\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(300 -\frac14\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(300-\frac{1}{4} \cdot 300\) </item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(300 \cdot \frac{3}{4}\), \(300 \cdot (1-\frac{1}{4})\), \(300-\frac{1}{4} \cdot
  300\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>A computer costs $800. It loses \(\frac14\) of its value every year after it is purchased.</item>
</list>
<para>Answer the following questions that would complete the table to show the value of the computer at the listed times.
</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Time (Years)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Value of Computer (Dollars)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
       0
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(t\)
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      The value of the computer, in dollars, when it is purchased. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>800
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      The value of the computer, in dollars, after 1 year.   </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>600
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      The value of the computer, in dollars, after 2 years. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 450
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>A computer costs $800. It loses \(\frac14\) of its value every year after it is purchased. Which of the following
    expressions would represent the value of the computer, in dollars, after \(t\) years?      </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    \(800 \cdot \frac{1}{4} \cdot t\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(800 \cdot \frac{3}{4} \cdot t\)
  </item>
  <item> \(800 \cdot (\frac{1}{4})^ t
    \)</item>
  <item> \(800 \cdot (\frac{3}{4})^t\)</item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(800 \cdot (\frac{3}{4})^t\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use your equation from question 3 to find \(v\) when \(t\) is 5. What does this value of \(v\) mean?
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
</list>  
  <list>
    <item>
      189.84; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $189.84.</item>
    <item>
      142.38; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $142.38.</item>
    <item>
      3,000; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $3,000.</item>
    <item>
      253.13; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $253.13.</item>
  </list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 189.84; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $189.84.
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>A piece of paper is folded into thirds multiple times. The area, \(A\), of the piece of paper in square inches,
    after \(n\) folds, is \(A=90 \cdot (\frac{1}{3})^n\).
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>
        What is the value of \(A\) when \(n=0\)? What does this mean in this situation? </item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 90. That is the area of the paper in square inches before it is folded.
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      How many folds are needed before the area is less than 1 square inch? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>The area of another piece of paper in square inches, after \(n\) folds, is given by \(A=100 \cdot
    (\frac{1}{2})^n\). What does the number 100 mean in this situation?
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    The area after the paper is folded
  </item>
  <item>
    The area before the paper is folded
  </item>
  <item>
    The number of folds to have an area of 0
  </item>
  <item>
    The growth factor of the situation </item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The area before the paper is folded
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>The area of another piece of paper in square inches, after \(n\) folds, is given by \(A=100 \cdot
    (\frac{1}{2})^n\). What does the number \(\frac12\) mean in this situation?
  </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    The area of the paper before it is folded
  </item>
  <item>
    The area of the paper after 1 fold
  </item>
  <item>
    The growth factor of the situation
  </item>
  <item>
    The initial value of the situation </item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The growth factor of the situation
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Lin has 13 mystery novels. Each month, she gets 2 more.  <emphasis>Select three</emphasis> expressions
    that represent the total number of mystery novels Lin has after 3 months.
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
</list>
<list>
  <item>
    \(13 + 2 + 2+ 2\)
  </item>
  <item>\(13 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2\)
  </item>
  <item>\(
    13 \cdot 8\)
  </item>
  <item>\(
    13 + 6\)
  </item>
  <item>\(
    19\)</item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(13 + 2 + 2+ 2\), \(13 + 6\), \(19\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph shows the amount of a chemical in a water sample. It is decreasing exponentially.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, time, hours, from 0 to 8 by 1's. Vertical axis, chemical, milligrams, from 0 to 1,200 by 200's. Plotted coordinates as follows: 0 comma 1,000, 1 comma 800. The next three points labeled A, B and C, with 4 more points plotted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/76ce25d2655e5f9a1aba167d1b970c3a1f3a5e67"/></media></figure></para>

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>      What are the coordinates of point \(A\)?
  </item></list>      
    <list>
        <item>
          \((0, 1000)\)
        </item>
        <item>\(
          (2, 640)\)
        </item>
        <item>\(
          (\frac{4}{5}, 1000)\)
        </item>
        <item>\(
          (1, 640)\) </item>
    </list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer</emphasis><emphasis>:</emphasis> \(
  (2, 640)\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the coordinates of point \(B\)? </item>
 </list>
 <list>
      <item>
        \((2, 640)\)
      </item>
      <item>
        \((3, 512)
        \)</item>
      <item>
        \((4, 410) 
        \)</item>
      <item>
        \((\frac{4}{5}, 512)
        \)</item>
  </list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer</emphasis><emphasis>:</emphasis> \((3, 512)\)
</para>

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      Which of the following equations would represent the chemical, \(C\), after time, \(t\), from the graph?
    </item>
  </list>
  <list>
      <item>
        \(C =1000+ \frac{4}{5}x\)
      </item>
      <item>
        \(C = 1000 \cdot \frac{4}{5} \cdot x\)
      </item>
      <item>\(
        C = 1000(\frac{4}{5})^x\)
      </item>
      <item>\(
        C = 1000+ \frac{4}{5}+x\)</item>
    
  </list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(C = 1000(\frac{4}{5})^x\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph shows the amount of a chemical in a water sample at different times after it was first measured.
    <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> statements that are true.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, time, hours, from 0 to 6 by 0 point 5's. Vertical axis, chemical, milligrams, from 0 to 2,400 by 400's. Plotted coordinates as follows: 0 comma 2,000, 1 comma 1,000, 2 comma 500, 3 comma 250, 4 comma 125."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ee1fba6e0c32ecada95e1b6b5f0718845179e0d3"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated"/>
<list>
  <item>
    The amount of the chemical in the water sample is decreasing exponentially.</item>
  <item>
    The amount of the chemical in the water sample is not decreasing exponentially.
  </item>
  <item>
    It is not possible to tell for certain whether the amount of the chemical is decreasing exponentially.
  </item>
  <item>
    When it was first measured, there were 2,000 mg of the chemical in the water sample.
  </item>
  <item>
    After 4 hours, there were 200 mg of the chemical in the water.
  </item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>  The amount of the chemical in the water sample is decreasing exponentially; When it was
  first measured, there were 2,000 mg of the chemical in the water sample. 
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item>The height of a plant in mm is 7. It doubles each week. <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> expressions that represent the
    height of the plant, in mm, after 4 weeks.
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated"/>
<list>
  <item>\(
    7+4 \cdot 2\)
  </item>
  <item>\(
    7 \cdot 2^4\)
  </item>
  <item>\(
    2+7^4\)
  </item>
  <item>\(
    7 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2\)
  </item>
  <item>\(7 \cdot 2 \cdot 4\)</item>
</list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(7 \cdot 2^4\), \(7 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2\)
</para></section>
